WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.174 --> 00:00:07.773
Hello.

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.475
My name is Eric Chow,

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.878
and I direct the UCSF Center for Advanced Technology.

00:00:11.879 --> 00:00:13.813
Today, I'm gonna give you a training video

00:00:13.814 --> 00:00:16.749
on how to operate the Agilent Bioanalyzer.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.385
This is the system used to QC nucleic acids

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:22.188
to go into next-generation sequencing library preps,

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:26.859
as well as check the quality of your final libraries before sequencing.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.895
This is the Bioanalyzer.

00:00:28.896 --> 00:00:32.365
And essentially, the system works by capillary electrophoresis.

00:00:32.366 --> 00:00:35.134
But instead of running samples through a long, thin capillary,

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:41.440
all of the separation occurs on these microfluidic chips, over here.

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:43.876
So, I'm going to open up this chip

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:46.145
and show you some more details of this chip.

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:47.947
The chip is actually made out of glass,

00:00:47.948 --> 00:00:50.917
and there are several wells on the chip.

00:00:50.918 --> 00:00:53.920
And this glass chip is embedded into a plastic frame.

00:00:53.921 --> 00:00:55.688
And on the back of this chip,

00:00:55.689 --> 00:00:58.057
you'll be able to see a whole bunch of different channels,

00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:00.393
and this is where all the different samples will run

00:01:00.394 --> 00:01:03.429
and get separated and then get detected.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.032
This chip, once it's prepared,

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:10.336
will go into this Bioanalyzer instrument.

00:01:10.337 --> 00:01:13.139
And this instrument has a location to hold the chip,

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.444
as well as an optical detector at the bottom to detect the passing nucleic acids,

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:20.379
and an electrode array on top.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.049
And this electrode array on top

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.451
is what causes the different samples to pass through the chip

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:29.155
and migrate towards that de... detector.

00:01:29.156 --> 00:01:32.491
So, we haven't done anything prepare the chip yet,

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.227
and we'll go through that next.

00:01:34.228 --> 00:01:36.762
So, with all the different kits for the Bioanalyzer,

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.898
you can analyze both RNA and DNA.

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:41.501
They come with a handy user guide

00:01:41.502 --> 00:01:43.736
that we'll essentially be following today.

00:01:43.737 --> 00:01:45.605
And in this user guide,

00:01:45.606 --> 00:01:49.842
there are sections where it tells you to prepare a gel-dye mixture.

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:52.311
This is something you'll have to do ahead of time,

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:54.146
and it takes about 30 minutes,

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:56.582
so this is something we've done before...

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.284
before we've shot this video.

00:01:58.285 --> 00:02:01.721
But all the reagents for the kits come in a small box like this

00:02:01.722 --> 00:02:03.523
that's kept in the cold room,

00:02:03.524 --> 00:02:05.925
and they all come with a pack of chips

00:02:05.926 --> 00:02:07.493
that are at room temperature.

00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:10.162
And so, once you open up this box

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:13.566
-- and today we're gonna be going over the DNA high sensitivity kits --

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:16.536
you'll see a set of reagents in here.

00:02:16.537 --> 00:02:20.706
And the reagents that you'll need to prepare the gel-dye mixture ahead of time

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:28.381
are a dye, the gel, and a spin filter.

00:02:28.382 --> 00:02:31.851
And what you'll do ahead of time is take a certain amount of the dye,

00:02:31.852 --> 00:02:34.187
add it to the gel and vortex it,

00:02:34.188 --> 00:02:37.790
and then you'll place this gel-dye mixture

00:02:37.791 --> 00:02:39.692
into this Eppendorf tube that has a spin filter in there,

00:02:39.693 --> 00:02:41.894
and you'll centrifuge this for about 30 minutes.

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:45.698
This will cause the gel-dye to form a nice even suspension.

00:02:45.699 --> 00:02:48.401
And when you're done, you'll throw away the filter,

00:02:48.402 --> 00:02:52.371
and keep the gel-dye in this Eppendorf tube at 4 degrees.

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:55.408
And you'll be able to use this gel-dye mixture several times,

00:02:55.409 --> 00:03:03.816
for several runs across many chips.

00:03:03.817 --> 00:03:07.486
So, what I have here is a tube of gel-dye that we've already prepared.

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:09.388
And this is what we'll be using today.

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:11.624
But before we start preparing the chip,

00:03:11.625 --> 00:03:13.726
I'm gonna demonstrate to you

00:03:13.727 --> 00:03:15.494
how to run the software.

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:18.197
The software that runs the Bioanalyzer

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:19.999
is the 2100 Expert software.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.034
When you open up the software,

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:24.604
it will automatically connect to the Bioanalyzer instrument.

00:03:24.605 --> 00:03:28.107
And on the left are a set of tabs...

00:03:28.108 --> 00:03:30.676
icons that you can use to navigate to different areas of the software.

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:32.545
To set up the run,

00:03:32.546 --> 00:03:35.081
you want to make sure you click on the instrument icon.

00:03:35.082 --> 00:03:37.650
And on the instrument icon,

00:03:37.651 --> 00:03:39.919
again you'll see a picture of the Bioanalyzer.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.421
And the first thing you want to do is select the assay that you're running.

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:45.825
Again, the Bioanalyzer can analyze both DNA and RNA.

00:03:45.826 --> 00:03:49.061
And if you're selecting DNA, you want to select the proper chip.

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:52.098
And today we're running the high sensitivity chip.

00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:55.034
For RNA, there are two different types of kits.

00:03:55.035 --> 00:03:58.271
There are these Nano and Pico kits,

00:03:58.272 --> 00:04:02.942
as well as a Small RNA kit if you want to run small RNA assays.

00:04:02.943 --> 00:04:05.211
But what's important about the Nano and Pico kits

00:04:05.212 --> 00:04:08.047
if you're analyzing RNA and want to get a RIN number

00:04:08.048 --> 00:04:10.249
-- this is an RNA integrity number --

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.085
you have to make sure that you select the total RNA options.

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:16.289
And this will cause the software to calculate a RIN number.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.691
If you select the mRNA options,

00:04:18.692 --> 00:04:21.160
you won't get a RIN number for your RNA.

00:04:21.161 --> 00:04:23.329
So, again, since we're running DNA high sensitivity,

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:27.533
we'll go to double-stranded DNA and select high sensitivity.

00:04:27.534 --> 00:04:31.137
Next, you have an option to select where your data gets saved to.

00:04:31.138 --> 00:04:36.108
It can save into a default folder or a custom folder that you select.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:38.477
After that, let the system know how many samples you'll be running.

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:40.680
On the high sensitivity DNA chip,

00:04:40.681 --> 00:04:42.381
you can run up to 11 samples.

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:44.517
Today, we'll only be running 2 samples.

00:04:44.518 --> 00:04:48.120
And at the bottom, you can give names to your different samples,

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:51.123
and you can label them down here.

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:56.729
And so, we'll just use sample A and sample B.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:00.733
So, now we're gonna set up the instrument and the chips to run your samples.

00:05:00.734 --> 00:05:03.069
Again, this is the Bioanalyzer,

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:07.106
and this head opens up and down to get the electrodes into a chip.

00:05:07.107 --> 00:05:11.177
If you need to remove your electrode, simply flip down a tab

00:05:11.178 --> 00:05:13.412
-- it's over here all the way to the bottom.

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.815
This will push out the electrode array by a little bit,

00:05:15.816 --> 00:05:17.850
and once it's out you can slide it back.

00:05:17.851 --> 00:05:19.685
And if you need to store this electrode array,

00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:22.188
store it upside down so that these electrodes

00:05:22.189 --> 00:05:26.225
don't get damaged by bumping up against surfaces.

00:05:26.226 --> 00:05:28.327
To put the electrode back into the system,

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:30.830
simply slide it back down

00:05:30.831 --> 00:05:32.665
and allow it to go down on its own.

00:05:32.666 --> 00:05:33.833
Don't force it in.

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:35.968
Once it has dropped to the lowest position,

00:05:35.969 --> 00:05:38.804
lift the lever up, and this will pull the electrode array in.

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:41.207
The next thing we're gonna do is wash the system.

00:05:41.208 --> 00:05:43.042
To wash the system,

00:05:43.043 --> 00:05:45.945
you want to use 350 microliters of nuclease-free water

00:05:45.946 --> 00:05:54.520
and place it into a wash chip.

00:05:54.521 --> 00:05:56.556
You can use any well of the wash chip,

00:05:56.557 --> 00:06:03.729
because all the wells are connected by a reservoir.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.833
To wash, simply place the wash chip on the chip holder

00:06:06.834 --> 00:06:09.302
and close the system,

00:06:09.303 --> 00:06:11.904
and let it sit for about 30 seconds to 1 minute.

00:06:11.905 --> 00:06:14.740
All this does is wash off any salts and debris

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:16.776
that might be on the surface of those electrodes.

00:06:16.777 --> 00:06:18.678
Once the wash is done,

00:06:18.679 --> 00:06:20.479
go ahead and remove the chip,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.615
and you can just dump out the water into the trash,

00:06:22.616 --> 00:06:29.689
and save the chip for future runs.

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:31.490
Now, we'll prepare our chip.

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:34.560
I've already opened this up from the plastic packaging,

00:06:34.561 --> 00:06:38.965
and we'll place this into the priming station.

00:06:38.966 --> 00:06:42.201
The chip sits down in here and doesn't really move.

00:06:42.202 --> 00:06:43.903
And on the priming station,

00:06:43.904 --> 00:06:46.205
it's important to note that this is made up...

00:06:46.206 --> 00:06:49.342
a plunger, and there is a holder to hold the plunger at different positions.

00:06:49.343 --> 00:06:52.144
And depending on which chip and assay you use,

00:06:52.145 --> 00:06:55.815
you'll need to make sure that you set the plunger to the proper setting.

00:06:55.816 --> 00:06:58.885
So, there's a top, middle, and bottom setting.

00:06:58.886 --> 00:07:00.486
For the high sensitivity DNA chips,

00:07:00.487 --> 00:07:02.421
we'll have to use this lower setting.

00:07:02.422 --> 00:07:05.491
And so, you just move the silver handle down

00:07:05.492 --> 00:07:08.027
until you get it to this lower location.

00:07:08.028 --> 00:07:11.831
And make sure it catches into the hold.

00:07:11.832 --> 00:07:14.066
So, now this is set at the low position,

00:07:14.067 --> 00:07:16.235
and we're ready to start adding our reagents.

00:07:16.236 --> 00:07:19.238
The first reagent we'll be using is the gel-dye mix

00:07:19.239 --> 00:07:20.773
that we prepared ahead of time.

00:07:20.774 --> 00:07:23.442
This has had the dye added to the gel

00:07:23.443 --> 00:07:25.678
and spun through a filter already.

00:07:25.679 --> 00:07:31.050
What we'll do first is add 9 microliters of the gel-dye mixture

00:07:31.051 --> 00:07:34.954
to the gel position that's marked with a dark circle on your chip.

00:07:34.955 --> 00:07:39.625
And this is gonna be the second from the bottom.

00:07:39.626 --> 00:07:41.227
When you add reagents to these chips,

00:07:41.228 --> 00:07:43.863
you want to make sure that you touch the bottom of the chip.

00:07:43.864 --> 00:07:45.731
You don't have to worry about marring them,

00:07:45.732 --> 00:07:47.333
because they're made out of glass,

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:53.840
so they're much harder than your pipette tips.

00:07:53.841 --> 00:07:56.509
The other thing you want to make sure that you don't do

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:58.744
is expel a bubble at the bottom of the chip,

00:07:58.745 --> 00:08:01.514
because that's where those capillaries and channels are.

00:08:01.515 --> 00:08:03.916
If you have an air bubble down there,

00:08:03.917 --> 00:08:07.920
it's gonna disrupt the flow of current.

00:08:07.921 --> 00:08:13.593
The next step is to bring the syringe up to the 1 mil mark

00:08:13.594 --> 00:08:18.464
and then snap the lid shut until you hear a click.

00:08:18.465 --> 00:08:22.401
Next, gently and steadily push the plunger all the way down

00:08:22.402 --> 00:08:26.439
until it catches the hold.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.208
And you want to set a timer for one minute.

00:08:29.209 --> 00:08:32.311
What's happening right now is the pressure in the syringe

00:08:32.312 --> 00:08:36.048
is forcing that gel-dye mixture through a capillary inside of the chip,

00:08:36.049 --> 00:08:39.285
and this is forming that separation channel

00:08:39.286 --> 00:08:40.953
that all the samples will go through.

00:08:40.954 --> 00:08:43.890
So, both the pressure and timing are important for the different chips,

00:08:43.891 --> 00:08:46.025
so make sure you consult your user guide

00:08:46.026 --> 00:08:52.365
if you're running something else besides the high sensitivity DNA chip.

00:08:52.366 --> 00:08:54.200
So, while this is running

00:08:54.201 --> 00:08:55.968
-- we have about another 30 seconds --

00:08:55.969 --> 00:08:59.939
one thing you can use to check your chips afterwards is

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.942
you want to make sure that you don't see any of the channels after pressurization,

00:09:02.943 --> 00:09:05.311
because now they've all been filled with liquids.

00:09:05.312 --> 00:09:08.147
Before we do this step, if you take a look at your chip,

00:09:08.148 --> 00:09:10.016
on the back,

00:09:10.017 --> 00:09:12.018
you can actually see all the channels if you hold it up to the light

00:09:12.019 --> 00:09:14.654
because of the glass-air interfaces that are present.

00:09:14.655 --> 00:09:18.057
But after priming, you shouldn't see those anymore,

00:09:18.058 --> 00:09:22.595
because they should all be fluid-filled.

00:09:22.596 --> 00:09:26.699
A minute is up, and we'll just release the plunger.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.802
What you want to see is the plunger

00:09:29.803 --> 00:09:33.105
going up past the half mil mark within a couple of seconds,

00:09:33.106 --> 00:09:36.275
and this indicates that no pressure has leaked out of the system.

00:09:36.276 --> 00:09:44.817
And so, now you just slowly bring the plunger back up to the 1 ml mark,

00:09:44.818 --> 00:09:49.288
and then release the plunger from the chip.

00:09:49.289 --> 00:09:51.757
Next, we'll add another 9 microliters of the gel-dye mixture

00:09:51.758 --> 00:09:54.694
to the other three gel positions on the chip.

00:09:54.695 --> 00:10:00.333
These are the other three wells on the right side of the chip.

00:10:00.334 --> 00:10:02.268
You can use the same pipette tip for all of these,

00:10:02.269 --> 00:10:05.471
and again, just remember not to expel any air bubbles

00:10:05.472 --> 00:10:22.355
towards the bottom of the... the bottom of the wells.

00:10:22.356 --> 00:10:26.459
Next, we're gonna add 5 microliters of a marker solution

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:30.863
to each of the sample wells and the ladder wells.

00:10:30.864 --> 00:10:34.500
These are the 12 wells to the left of the chip.

00:10:34.501 --> 00:10:39.205
So, we don't add these to the gel wells that we've added material to already.

00:10:39.206 --> 00:10:43.509
And you can use the same pipette for all of these as well.

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:45.945
And this just contains an upper and lower marker

00:10:45.946 --> 00:10:47.980
that can be used as reference standards

00:10:47.981 --> 00:10:57.023
to compare your sample to the ladder.

00:10:57.024 --> 00:11:00.159
So, even if you're only running one or two samples,

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:01.894
or not a full 11 samples,

00:11:01.895 --> 00:11:04.230
you still have to add marker to all of the lanes,

00:11:04.231 --> 00:11:19.645
or to all of the wells.

00:11:19.646 --> 00:11:22.949
In the next step, we'll add one microliter of the ladder

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:42.068
to the ladder well, and this is marked on the chip.

00:11:42.069 --> 00:11:43.703
Now we can add your samples.

00:11:43.704 --> 00:11:47.306
And again, there are references to the well number

00:11:47.307 --> 00:12:12.832
that correspond to the sample numbers on the chip.

00:12:12.833 --> 00:12:15.668
I only have two samples, so I'm now done,

00:12:15.669 --> 00:12:19.205
but you can proceed with up to 11 samples on this chip.

00:12:19.206 --> 00:12:22.074
The next step is to mix the contents of the...

00:12:22.075 --> 00:12:25.444
of the marker and the samples with the ladder in the chip,

00:12:25.445 --> 00:12:29.782
and to do this we use a special vortexer that comes with every Bioanalyzer.

00:12:29.783 --> 00:12:32.518
So, just make sure it's set to the proper set point,

00:12:32.519 --> 00:12:34.787
and then allow it to mix.

00:12:34.788 --> 00:12:39.292
And make sure you set a timer so that you have...

00:12:39.293 --> 00:12:40.726
so you know when to stop.

00:12:40.727 --> 00:12:42.628
And again, to get the time for the different assays,

00:12:42.629 --> 00:12:46.799
just consult your user guide.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:49.635
Alright, times up.

00:12:49.636 --> 00:12:51.737
Now the chip is prepared and ready to run.

00:12:51.738 --> 00:12:55.708
What we want to do is go ahead and place this into the Bioanalyzer system,

00:12:55.709 --> 00:12:58.711
close the lid,

00:12:58.712 --> 00:13:02.114
and then once the system detects the chip

00:13:02.115 --> 00:13:04.917
you'll be able to click on the start button to begin the run.

00:13:04.918 --> 00:13:08.487
A run of a full chip will generally take about 45 minutes.

00:13:08.488 --> 00:13:10.990
And it's a good idea to stick around for a few minutes,

00:13:10.991 --> 00:13:13.226
'til you see the ladder run,

00:13:13.227 --> 00:13:17.330
just to make sure the run has started okay before you take off.

00:13:17.331 --> 00:13:19.465
Now, our second and last sample is running.

00:13:19.466 --> 00:13:22.001
What you see here is a raw trace for the Bioanalyzer,

00:13:22.002 --> 00:13:24.904
and this is analyzing fluorescence that's being detected

00:13:24.905 --> 00:13:27.773
as the sample passes through the capillary.

00:13:27.774 --> 00:13:30.776
On the low end we have the lower marker,

00:13:30.777 --> 00:13:33.246
and over here we have the upper marker.

00:13:33.247 --> 00:13:38.784
These are used as internal references to compare the sample to the ladder that was run first.

00:13:38.785 --> 00:13:40.553
And in the middle here is our library.

00:13:40.554 --> 00:13:42.688
This is a high concentration sample

00:13:42.689 --> 00:13:45.224
that is roughly at around 25 nanomolar.

00:13:45.225 --> 00:13:48.761
And so, this is why this smear is so large.

00:13:48.762 --> 00:13:51.297
You'll notice that the marker peaks are pretty sharp,

00:13:51.298 --> 00:13:53.699
because they're a defined size,

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:55.368
and libraries tend to have a spread to them,

00:13:55.369 --> 00:13:58.437
because they come in a variety of sizes.

00:13:58.438 --> 00:14:01.607
They're not one specific base pair length.

00:14:01.608 --> 00:14:04.644
And so, now the run is finishing,

00:14:04.645 --> 00:14:07.813
and I'll show you how to analyze and export the data.

00:14:07.814 --> 00:14:09.982
So, now that that screen has disappeared,

00:14:09.983 --> 00:14:11.584
the run is done.

00:14:11.585 --> 00:14:14.353
To access our data, we come back over to the left-hand side

00:14:14.354 --> 00:14:17.723
and click on the data icon.

00:14:17.724 --> 00:14:19.692
If you have several runs from beforehand,

00:14:19.693 --> 00:14:21.661
you'll see the other chips here.

00:14:21.662 --> 00:14:23.362
The newest one will be at the bottom,

00:14:23.363 --> 00:14:25.097
which is our sample.

00:14:25.098 --> 00:14:27.500
And if we click on that chip,

00:14:27.501 --> 00:14:30.803
we'll get some details about the chip,

00:14:30.804 --> 00:14:32.605
the assay properties, and then,

00:14:32.606 --> 00:14:34.173
if you want to take a look at the raw traces,

00:14:34.174 --> 00:14:36.509
you click on the electropherogram.

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:40.413
And this will show you the trace for... either at one sample at a time

00:14:40.414 --> 00:14:45.751
-- so, this is our first sample, and this was our second sample --

00:14:45.752 --> 00:14:48.321
or you can look at all samples at once.

00:14:48.322 --> 00:14:52.625
And here's sample A and sample B, and down here is the ladder.

00:14:52.626 --> 00:14:58.297
And so, if we click on the ladder, it has a series of peaks

00:14:58.298 --> 00:15:00.199
that are present in that ladder sample,

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.802
and the ladder also contained the marker that we added,

00:15:02.803 --> 00:15:04.971
the 5 microliters of reagents,

00:15:04.972 --> 00:15:09.141
and those are this 35 base pair and roughly 10 kb base pair fragments

00:15:09.142 --> 00:15:11.844
that are present in all samples.

00:15:11.845 --> 00:15:16.282
And so, the ladder is used as a reference, because we know the sizes,

00:15:16.283 --> 00:15:18.518
and we correlate it with the time that it takes for those samples

00:15:18.519 --> 00:15:20.486
to come through the chip,

00:15:20.487 --> 00:15:22.555
and we have these internal references

00:15:22.556 --> 00:15:25.124
that we'll use to compare each of the samples.

00:15:25.125 --> 00:15:28.661
So, now if you click on sample A, it'll also have those 35 and 10 kb markers

00:15:28.662 --> 00:15:34.767
that are used to generate the sizing of these samples.

00:15:34.768 --> 00:15:37.570
So, down here on the electropherogram

00:15:37.571 --> 00:15:38.938
there are a couple of things you can do.

00:15:38.939 --> 00:15:40.640
You can set something called a region table,

00:15:40.641 --> 00:15:42.842
and for next-gen sequencing libraries

00:15:42.843 --> 00:15:44.944
you typically set these around 200 base pairs

00:15:44.945 --> 00:15:46.646
up to 1 kb.

00:15:46.647 --> 00:15:48.281
And that's displayed here.

00:15:48.282 --> 00:15:51.851
And what this will do is it'll analyze only the material that shows up within this range.

00:15:51.852 --> 00:15:54.754
And this will give you a rough concentration, in picomoles.

00:15:54.755 --> 00:15:58.691
So, this is a little over 4000 picomolar concentration,

00:15:58.692 --> 00:16:02.028
or roughly 4 nanomolar.

00:16:02.029 --> 00:16:04.163
It'll also give you an average size,

00:16:04.164 --> 00:16:06.165
which is useful for some downstream QC'ing steps.

00:16:06.166 --> 00:16:09.936
And so, this library has an average size of 320 base pairs.

00:16:09.937 --> 00:16:14.040
Our other high concentration sample, again,

00:16:14.041 --> 00:16:16.709
is also being analyzed in this smear region.

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:20.546
And what you can see is that this is a much higher concentration library.

00:16:20.547 --> 00:16:24.550
This is measuring it at roughly 17 nanomolar,

00:16:24.551 --> 00:16:30.156
and the average size is 491 base pairs.

00:16:30.157 --> 00:16:33.593
Sometimes the Bioanalyzer won't detect your marker peaks correctly,

00:16:33.594 --> 00:16:36.596
and you'll see a red flag or a red mark down here

00:16:36.597 --> 00:16:38.331
in the simulated gel image.

00:16:38.332 --> 00:16:40.633
This is something that's easy to correct

00:16:40.634 --> 00:16:43.870
by going to the peak table tab, over here.

00:16:43.871 --> 00:16:46.806
So, for instance, on this sample B,

00:16:46.807 --> 00:16:49.108
we have this minor peak that showed up.

00:16:49.109 --> 00:16:53.012
Sometimes this minor peak might get misidentified as a marker.

00:16:53.013 --> 00:16:55.348
And on this peak table,

00:16:55.349 --> 00:16:58.384
what you can do is you can highlight over these peaks,

00:16:58.385 --> 00:17:00.419
and once you see a bullseye appear,

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:04.690
you can right click and manually set them as the lower or upper marker.

00:17:04.691 --> 00:17:06.959
And so, for instance,

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:09.295
if I wanted to call this 29 base pair peak

00:17:09.296 --> 00:17:13.399
my 35 base pair standard, or internal reference,

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:16.102
I can right click on it and manually set the lower marker.

00:17:16.103 --> 00:17:18.938
And this will recalculate the size of everything.

00:17:18.939 --> 00:17:23.142
So, now you can see that the software has now called this my 35 base pair peak,

00:17:23.143 --> 00:17:26.479
and it's calling this marker a 46 base pair peak.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:30.883
So, this isn't what I actually want, so I'll go ahead and change it back.

00:17:30.884 --> 00:17:33.119
And hover over my actual lower marker,

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:39.225
see the bullseye, right click, and set lower marker.

00:17:39.226 --> 00:17:43.729
So, once your data looks like it's all been set and is...

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:45.798
all the peaks have been properly called,

00:17:45.799 --> 00:17:48.501
you can export this as a PDF

00:17:48.502 --> 00:17:53.206
by clicking the printer button on the toolbar.

00:17:53.207 --> 00:17:55.541
And so, sometimes it'll ask you if you want to save any changes.

00:17:55.542 --> 00:17:59.712
And so, I said, yes, I want to save those peak changes that I made.

00:17:59.713 --> 00:18:02.381
A window will show up,

00:18:02.382 --> 00:18:04.584
and it'll give you options of what type of items to select.

00:18:04.585 --> 00:18:06.752
We just leave this as the default.

00:18:06.753 --> 00:18:09.021
You can tell it to print all wells

00:18:09.022 --> 00:18:10.756
or just the wells that you have samples.

00:18:10.757 --> 00:18:13.292
And for us, you only had two samples.

00:18:13.293 --> 00:18:18.431
And then you have options to export this as a PDF or an HTML... HTML file,

00:18:18.432 --> 00:18:20.867
and to select the directory that you want to export this to.

00:18:20.868 --> 00:18:23.069
So, I'm just gonna save it to the desktop

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:24.537
to make it easier to find.

00:18:24.538 --> 00:18:28.274
And you have options to put four sheets per page or one sheet per page.

00:18:28.275 --> 00:18:35.114
So, I'll go ahead and save this.

00:18:35.115 --> 00:18:37.416
And if we go to the desktop,

00:18:37.417 --> 00:18:42.021
we should be able to see our PDF file,

00:18:42.022 --> 00:18:45.024
and that should be here.

00:18:45.025 --> 00:18:46.759
So, if you double-click on this,

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:48.728
you'll see a summary report on the first page.

00:18:48.729 --> 00:18:50.696
And as you scroll down,

00:18:50.697 --> 00:18:54.300
you'll see the ladder, over here,

00:18:54.301 --> 00:18:58.171
and you want to see nice, tight, sharp bands, or peaks, on the ladder.

00:18:58.172 --> 00:19:01.274
And then you'll see the samples.

00:19:01.275 --> 00:19:06.312
And these will include some extra information down at the bottom.

00:19:06.313 --> 00:19:08.481
And so, most people will want to save the PDF,

00:19:08.482 --> 00:19:11.150
because this is the file they can open on their own computers.

00:19:11.151 --> 00:19:13.786
You can't open the actual Bioanalyzer raw files,

00:19:13.787 --> 00:19:17.056
these XAD files, because it requires the proprietary software,

00:19:17.057 --> 00:19:19.725
so you definitely want to export and save that PDF.

00:19:19.726 --> 00:19:23.529
And generally this PDF is what you would send off to your sequencing facility,

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:27.800
if you're getting a sample sequenced.

00:19:27.801 --> 00:19:30.069
So, now that the run is completed,

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:32.505
the last thing we have to do is wash the system.

00:19:32.506 --> 00:19:34.540
So, the first thing you want to do is pull out the old chip,

00:19:34.541 --> 00:19:41.380
set it aside -- you can throw it away, it's unusable right now --

00:19:41.381 --> 00:19:50.122
and then take another wash chip and fill it again with 350 microliters of nuclease-free water.

00:19:50.123 --> 00:19:51.891
And then place a chip in the system

00:19:51.892 --> 00:19:54.393
and close the lid, and let it sit for about 30 seconds to one minute.

00:19:54.394 --> 00:19:56.562
And it's really important that you do this afterwards,

00:19:56.563 --> 00:19:59.398
because we want to clean the electrodes of any gel and salts

00:19:59.399 --> 00:20:02.301
that are gonna be on the electrodes from running the chip.

00:20:02.302 --> 00:20:04.370
It's not good to leave a chip in there long-term,

00:20:04.371 --> 00:20:06.405
because those things can dry on those electrodes

00:20:06.406 --> 00:20:09.575
and cause problems for the next user.

00:20:09.576 --> 00:20:12.478
The other point is that it's really important that you remove the wash chip.

00:20:12.479 --> 00:20:14.347
If you leave it in there,

00:20:14.348 --> 00:20:17.316
the water will start to evaporate

00:20:17.317 --> 00:20:19.018
and cause condensation inside of the electrode array,

00:20:19.019 --> 00:20:21.721
so there are a lot of electronics up above

00:20:21.722 --> 00:20:24.624
that we don't want to get moisture on.

00:20:24.625 --> 00:20:26.592
So, now that 30 seconds is up,

00:20:26.593 --> 00:20:28.060
we remove the wash chip,

00:20:28.061 --> 00:20:30.696
we can get rid of the water,

00:20:30.697 --> 00:20:33.699
set it aside, and then just close the system, and you're done.

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:51.285
Thanks for watching.

